WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:01.610
In the previous section of the training,

1
00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.969
we focused on how Stanford uses external market information to

2
00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:08.840
inform compensation decisions that are equitable and market competitive.

3
00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.239
In this next section,

4
00:00:10.449 --> 00:00:12.489
we will discuss how we consider and maintain

5
00:00:12.489 --> 00:00:15.350
pay equity across Stanford through internal comparability.

6
00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:20.090
Internal comparability focuses on employees in the same or similar jobs.

7
00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.149
So,

8
00:00:21.489 --> 00:00:25.709
let's take a moment to understand how Stanford determines if jobs are similar.

9
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:28.430
Stanford uses something called a job structure.

10
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.840
Which is a framework that enables us to understand how all jobs relate to each other.

11
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.380
Our job structure includes 7 factors

12
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.669
job family,

13
00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:37.950
job function,

14
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.270
job level,

15
00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:40.790
job description,

16
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.189
job code,

17
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:43.439
job title,

18
00:00:43.799 --> 00:00:44.720
and position.

19
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.450
Today we're going to focus on the 1st 4,

20
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.180
as you see listed on the slide,

21
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.279
which are the most relevant for internal comparability across all of Stanford.

22
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.590
Similar jobs will have the same job family,

23
00:00:58.029 --> 00:00:58.909
job function.

24
00:00:59.319 --> 00:01:00.400
And job level.

25
00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:03.680
There will also be close similarities between the job descriptions,

26
00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.959
including expectations around knowledge,

27
00:01:06.279 --> 00:01:06.779
skills

28
00:01:06.879 --> 00:01:08.709
and abilities required for the job

29
00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.300
and similar core duties and responsibilities.

30
00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:12.519
As a manager,

31
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.180
you will likely come across Stanford's job structure

32
00:01:15.519 --> 00:01:17.839
when you are looking at a job function matrix

33
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.779
shown here on the bottom of the slide.

34
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:22.879
Your HR team can help you access a matrix like this to

35
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:25.639
you when you are establishing a new position on your team.

36
00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:28.589
Looking to reclassify an existing position

37
00:01:28.769 --> 00:01:32.309
or simply needing a refresher on the details of the current positions on your team?

38
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.790
You can also access it through the job description library,

39
00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:38.150
accessible through Cardinal at work.

40
00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.069
This information may also be useful

41
00:01:40.069 --> 00:01:42.430
to you when considering internal comparability

42
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.089
as it can also help you understand differences between

43
00:01:45.089 --> 00:01:47.629
jobs at different levels within the same job function.

44
00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:51.959
A job function matrix will include roles within a specific job family,

45
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.199
for example,

46
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.470
finance

47
00:01:54.769 --> 00:01:56.349
and a specific job function.

48
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.550
Like accounting,

49
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.980
the job function matrix then provides detailed information

50
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.720
for the various job levels that fall under that specific function.

51
00:02:04.919 --> 00:02:05.949
In this example,

52
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.020
using the job function accounting,

53
00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.940
There are 5 job levels denoted by grades.

54
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.470
For each job level

55
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:13.289
or grade,

56
00:02:13.529 --> 00:02:17.130
there is a job title and key elements that comprise a job description.

57
00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:19.509
Job descriptions tend to include

58
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.130
job function summary,

59
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.270
job purpose,

60
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:23.759
core duties,

61
00:02:24.009 --> 00:02:26.050
required education and experience,

62
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.630
required knowledge,

63
00:02:28.009 --> 00:02:29.199
skills and abilities,

64
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.289
required certifications and licenses.

65
00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.929
It can also include factors such as scope,

66
00:02:34.429 --> 00:02:34.889
impact,

67
00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:36.080
Authority,

68
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:36.979
leadership,

69
00:02:37.559 --> 00:02:38.750
level of complexity,

70
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.580
and if the role is strategic or operational.

71
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.630
Here's where internal comparability from a

72
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.720
compensation perspective comes into play.

73
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.860
The Stanford University HR compensation team

74
00:02:49.119 --> 00:02:52.259
uses the information within the job function matrix,

75
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.259
primarily the job descriptions.

76
00:02:54.649 --> 00:02:57.070
To benchmark each job to the marketplace.

77
00:02:57.289 --> 00:03:01.720
This is done by looking to match each job to job data that has the same scope,

78
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:02.720
core duties,

79
00:03:03.399 --> 00:03:03.729
education,

80
00:03:04.169 --> 00:03:04.710
experience,

81
00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:05.289
etc.

82
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.679
That is how we attach a job market salary range to a job.

83
00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:12.229
Similar jobs will have similar job market salary ranges.

84
00:03:12.339 --> 00:03:15.199
And jobs with different levels and job descriptions

85
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.529
will have different job market ranges,

86
00:03:17.899 --> 00:03:19.240
meaning they are considered

87
00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:24.000
distinctly different jobs when considering internal comparability.

88
00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.169
It's important to keep in mind that Stanford's

89
00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:29.119
job structure and market benchmarking is about jobs,

90
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.119
not people,

91
00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:32.699
and jobs reflect the business needs of Stanford.

92
00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.559
Benchmarking takes place annually and salary ranges are adjusted accordingly.

93
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.610
This process is agnostic of the individual filling a job

94
00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.429
and focuses on the details included within the job description.

95
00:03:42.649 --> 00:03:44.889
Now that we have some understanding of the context

96
00:03:44.889 --> 00:03:46.710
of our jobs and how they are organized,

97
00:03:47.009 --> 00:03:50.289
let's take a closer look at internal comparability and pay equity.

98
00:03:50.449 --> 00:03:53.309
Internal comparability comes into play when hiring an employee

99
00:03:53.309 --> 00:03:56.000
and when considering pay adjustments for existing employees.

100
00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:57.210
In both cases,

101
00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.679
you will work with HR to ensure compensation decisions are

102
00:04:00.679 --> 00:04:04.449
equitable and internally comparable for your role as a manager.

103
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.630
It will be helpful for you to understand

104
00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.529
the factors that are considered for internal comparability,

105
00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:11.119
so you can explain them to your team.

106
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.789
Internal comparability focuses on pay equity between employees

107
00:04:14.789 --> 00:04:17.390
in the same or similar roles within Stanford.

108
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:18.769
This considers

109
00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:19.820
knowledge skills,

110
00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:20.700
experience,

111
00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.500
abilities,

112
00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:21.988
work,

113
00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:22.859
location,

114
00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:24.970
time in the position and performance.

115
00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.390
The balance of these aspects informs equitable

116
00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.429
offers of compensation and equitable pay adjustments.

117
00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:35.470
This is a key part of delivering a fair and competitive staff compensation program.

118
00:04:35.619 --> 00:04:36.309
Typically,

119
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.450
knowledge,

120
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:38.450
skills,

121
00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:39.690
experience

122
00:04:39.989 --> 00:04:41.190
and work location

123
00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:44.170
are heavily considered for internal comparability

124
00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:45.769
when hiring a new employee.

125
00:04:46.029 --> 00:04:48.010
Once an employee has joined Stanford,

126
00:04:48.309 --> 00:04:50.329
the focus of internal comparability

127
00:04:50.549 --> 00:04:52.570
centers more on employee performance

128
00:04:52.989 --> 00:04:57.049
as Stanford's philosophy is to recognize and reward high performance.

129
00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:57.950
For example,

130
00:04:58.279 --> 00:05:02.239
If you have two employees who have the same experience,

131
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.279
skills,

132
00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:04.470
knowledge,

133
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:05.640
abilities,

134
00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:07.079
work location,

135
00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:08.640
time and position

136
00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:09.790
and performance,

137
00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:12.420
you would expect their pay to be very close.

138
00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:13.140
However,

139
00:05:13.399 --> 00:05:15.600
often there are differences across these aspects

140
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.750
when you compare employees in similar roles,

141
00:05:17.959 --> 00:05:21.019
and these differences influence equitable pay decisions.

142
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.140
In particular,

143
00:05:22.359 --> 00:05:24.149
if employee performance differs,

144
00:05:24.339 --> 00:05:25.540
as it often does.

145
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.420
There will be reasonable differences in compensation.

146
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.459
Your HR partner will help you when considering internal comparability

147
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.019
and equitable compensation for new and existing employees.

148
00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:37.679
Let's take a moment to look at how

149
00:05:37.679 --> 00:05:41.779
Stanford's location-based pay approach factors into internal comparability.

150
00:05:41.959 --> 00:05:43.070
In the previous section,

151
00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.600
we looked at location-based pay from the perspective of extra

152
00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.109
Market competitiveness.

153
00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.730
It's important to understand

154
00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.149
that it also supports internal pay equity

155
00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:56.029
when making compensation decisions for employees in similar roles

156
00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.880
who are based in different locations.

157
00:05:58.049 --> 00:06:01.190
Let's assume you're hiring two new employees to fill similar roles

158
00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.559
and you have two equally qualified candidates.

159
00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:04.970
Both have the same knowledge,

160
00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:05.410
skills,

161
00:06:05.489 --> 00:06:07.790
ability and experience coming into the job.

162
00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:08.570
However,

163
00:06:08.809 --> 00:06:11.200
one will be working at a Stanford work location in

164
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.859
Palo Alto and one will be working remotely in Salem,

165
00:06:13.959 --> 00:06:14.529
Arkansas.

166
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.130
At first,

167
00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.929
it may feel tempting to offer them the same salary.

168
00:06:19.109 --> 00:06:22.850
This would not be in alignment with Stanford's location-based pay policy

169
00:06:22.989 --> 00:06:26.850
as pay must be appropriate for the location where the work is being performed.

170
00:06:26.989 --> 00:06:30.570
If both employees were paid $138,000

171
00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.540
which is the midpoint of the jobs market salary range in Palo Alto,

172
00:06:34.859 --> 00:06:37.549
this would create a circumstance of inequitable pay.

173
00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:38.350
Remember,

174
00:06:38.709 --> 00:06:41.640
Equitable pay does not necessarily mean equal pay.

175
00:06:41.869 --> 00:06:42.700
In this case,

176
00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.190
the location in which the work is being performed

177
00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.070
is the key differentiator between these two employees,

178
00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:51.850
and each work location has a specific job market salary range

179
00:06:52.019 --> 00:06:53.970
based on cost of labor for that zone.

180
00:06:54.119 --> 00:06:57.339
The cost of labor is higher in Palo Alto than it is in Salem,

181
00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:57.890
Arkansas.

182
00:06:58.489 --> 00:07:01.940
Which influences the appropriate compensation for each employee.

183
00:07:02.179 --> 00:07:05.809
Equitable pay would mean these two employees are both paid at the midpoint of

184
00:07:05.809 --> 00:07:08.320
their appropriate market range rather than both

185
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.320
employees being paid the same dollar amount.

186
00:07:10.619 --> 00:07:12.040
If the employee in Salem

187
00:07:12.179 --> 00:07:16.959
was paid $138,000 instead of $83,000

188
00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.019
which is the midpoint of the market range for zone five.

189
00:07:20.859 --> 00:07:24.559
This employee would be receiving an inflated salary that takes them outside

190
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:27.700
of the appropriate market range for their work location.

191
00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:32.760
Your HR partner will work with you to apply Stanford's location-based pay approach

192
00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:36.359
to compensation offers and to navigate internal comparability.

193
00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.040
This includes when an existing employee moves work locations

194
00:07:40.339 --> 00:07:42.820
as a compensation adjustment might be appropriate.